instructions:
- Rewrite this script using ES6+ syntax;
- Use defensive programming techniques for preventing UI crashes caused by the script, such as proper nullish coalescence, falsish guard clauses and try/catches. IF the procedure executes a critical change in the page: -> query for bootstrap's UI link existence in the page is sucessful and mount a boostrap toast alerting the user the procedure has failed; if the query fails, just use javascript's native alert call;
- Do not include comments on more than one newline between lines;
- Do not include console.[log method] in the procedures for route checking, ONLY for broad loading failures (like library loading failures);
- The toast/alert of error should only be shown to the user if he interacts with it (in a click trigger for common events or pointerup for throttle-sensitive procedures, such as those that trigger stateful http methods or fetch large amounts of data from get | head, DO NOT add both types of listeners, this means to pick only one according to the context). Remember to include dataset-[check alias] (using getAttribute and setAttribute in association with reusable variables to represent repeatead strings) to prevent rerendering from adding multiple times the same listener;
- Remember to include checks to cleanup the listeners if the element is removed from the DOM, such as using MutationObserver or similar techniques;
- Remember to include checks to prevent appending of new DOM nodes if they already exist, such as using querySelector or similar techniques;
- The message shown in the toast/alert should be localized through th creation and usage of a dictionary called "translations", that has the structure translations[...{ar, da, de, en, es, fr, he, it, ja, nl, pl, pt, pt-br, ru, tr, zh}][{...'[context alias]_unavailable'}] using something like this example:
		const lang = (window.sessionStorage.getItem("erp-np-lang") || document.documentElement.lang || "en").toLowerCase();
	  const msg = window.translations[lang]?.[argumented key] ?? '# ERROR';
- The translations should be stored in a separate synchronous script declaring the complete dict for window.translations of that page;
- The procedure for the translation fetch is always the same, besides the key usages. If more than one script is using the same pattern, you can declare the procedure as a reusable function in the lexical scope:
	```
					const errFb = "# ERROR";
					const dataClientLocalized = "data-client-localized";
					const dataGuardMsg = "data-guard-msg";
					let msg = errFb;
					if (
						el.getAttribute("data-sv-localized") === "true" ||
						el.getAttribute(dataClientLocalized) === "true"
					)
						msg = el.getAttribute(dataGuardMsg) || errFb;
					else {
						let lang = (
							window.sessionStorage.getItem("erp-np-lang") ||
							document.documentElement.lang ||
							"en"
						)
							.toLowerCase()
							.replace(/_/g, "-");
						lang = lang === "pt-br" ? lang : lang.slice(0, 2);
						const msgKey = /* HERE THE KEY VALUE */;
						msg =
							window.translations?.[lang]?.[msgKey] ||
							el.getAttribute(dataGuardMsg) ||
							window.translations?.["en"]?.[msgKey] ||
							errFb;
						if (msg !== errFb) {
							el.setAttribute(dataGuardMsg, msg);
							el.setAttribute(dataClientLocalized, "true");
						}
					}
	```
- Don't try to circunvent jquerys usage, even though it's surely a legacy approach;
